ents  here are already used to dealing with  the threat of a tsunami caused by an  earthquake  they regularly practice evacuating and  fleeing to higher ground on their school  buses  now the tribe is building an entirely  new school on top of the hill far away  from those rising ocean waters and while  it will be bigger and better than the  current school  some students can't imagine not seeing  the very water now forcing them to flee  it's sad then it's going to be really  different this is where a lot of us grew  up it means a lot to us  native tribes from alaska to the  southwest to florida are facing severe  climate impacts in large part because  they were relocated onto some of the  country's least desirable land the  infrastructure bill just signed by  president biden includes 216 million  dollars for climate resilience and  adaptation for tribal nations including  some funds for relocation our main focus  is definitely the kids and the elders  tony foster is a member of the tribal  council while government grants are  paying for most of the new school  construction he isn't sure if they will  get funding for the rest of the tribe to  move and build new homes on land near  the school  so it's not as simple as saying it's  time to move to higher ground no it's  really difficult for all of us to say we  have to leave  our home because of the climate i'll  pick up the kids later and penn charles  fears that day will come i'm not going  to move unless mother nature makes me  move but to honor her ancestors she says  she'll hold out as long as she can  you know we signed over so much land to  stay here it'd be tough to give up our  land down here it'd be like taking  taking our heartbeat and giving it a zap  and would be leaving our heart here  within our areas that we all grew up in  land this tribe never chose but a home  they may now have to leave behind  for cbs mornings ben tracy on the  olympic peninsula  just unbelievably beautiful landscape  there and if the projections are true  it's not the first story we're going to  do about people giving up coastal  property because of climate change very  personal isn't it yeah it sure is your  heartbeat and giving it a zap right  about that

